### Diagnosis (read-only; outputs in `run_log/studyb_diag/`; no file in PKG was changed)

Observed in `run_log/studyb_diag/renamer2.log`, for the names that failed all 100 tries of `OpaqueNamer.fit` in `src/ngraph/rename.py`:

| Repo | Name | Composed new name | Why rejected | Distinct new names in 100 tries |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| matplotlib/matplotlib | `''` (empty) | `b` | already a name in the repo (`taken_names`) | 1 |
| sympy/sympy | `a11` | `f11` | already a name in the repo (`taken_names`) | 1 |
| sympy/sympy | `a1_` | `f1_` | already a name in the repo (`taken_names`) | 1 |
| sympy/sympy | `a2_` | `f2_` | already a name in the repo (`taken_names`) | 1 |

Cause, inferred from those numbers and from reading `fit` and `_draw`: the name's one-letter sub-token is redrawn after each rejection, but the redraw returned the same letter every time. That fits the 26 one-letter pseudo-words all being in use, so the only free letter is the one just released. `_draw` returns that letter and never reaches its longer-word fallback, so `fit` repeats the same rejected name until it gives up. I did not instrument `_pseudo_word` to prove the pool is full.

Why matplotlib reaches this path: `collect_names` takes the top-level package from `mod.split('.')[0]`. The file `.circleci/fetch_doc_logs.py` gets the module name `.circleci.fetch_doc_logs`, so the first component is the empty string and it joins `names.identity`. `shape('')` gives the placeholder sub-token `x`. xarray and scikit-learn also have the empty name and pass, so the empty name alone is not fatal. sympy has the empty name too, but its reported failures are `a11`, `a1_` and `a2_`.

